── THE TABLE ABOVE WAS RAGGED UNTIL ROUND 50, and it is worth saying how it got that way. A column was
added per round and only the rows a round had touched were extended, so most rows had fewer cells than
the header — a reader lining up "r44" against a short row was reading a DIFFERENT round's number. It is
now one cell per column, with `—` where a round did not record that file, and the per-file cells come
from the kept reading of the round named in the column (r46 and r49 use their FINAL reading, which is
what the prose quotes). A table nobody re-reads is the same defect as a number nobody re-reads.

── ROUNDS 64–65: A FAILED PREVIEW, AND A REAL DEFECT FOUND BY ASSERTING THE PROMISE (§72) ──
The compile-failure path had a guard with a measured reason: a first commit that fails must leave no
project behind, or the id is burned (`store.exists()` stays true, reading it throws
`REVISION_ID_INVALID`, retrying the same id throws `PROJECT_EXISTS`). The PREVIEW-failure path — a render
that dies after a successful compile — kept the half-project, because the guard lived in the other catch
only. Two checks written against the PROMISE ("a refused first commit costs a message, not your work",
and its retry) failed and found it; the rule now lives in one method both paths call.

`contract/revision-preview-failure.test.mjs` reached 10 checks and the product total went 305 → 272.

── ROUNDS 58–60: THE HOST'S SMALL REFUSALS, AND THE READING THEY BOUGHT (§69) ──
The host's last refusals that need no Blender: every artifact content type the UI can open (including the
octet-stream default), an artifact read with no path or with an absolute one, a delivery for a profile
the revision does not declare (the message lists the ones it does), a delivery with no checkpoint
anywhere, the "newest deliverable" rule, a resume of a job that already finished, and an export of a
project with no job. `host/lib/index.js` 132 → 89, and the product total 349 → 305.

Two branches are NOT reached and are named in the test rather than left looking covered:
`resumeRenderJob`'s "already running in this Host" refusal (the path continues into a renderer this
fixture does not build) and the `delivery.status === 'published'` rule — removing that rule SURVIVES the
mutation run, because on every candidate the file writes the next rule (enough completed frames) returns
the same record. The two overlap on that input, and telling them apart needs a job that published and
then lost frames, which the product does not produce.

── ROUNDS 56–57: THE THIN LAYERS UNDER EVERYTHING (§68) ──
The last scattered dark lines in three files every other test depends on: `paths.js` (a write that fails
removes its temporary file, a missing document that the caller said may not be missing, a document that
exists but cannot be read, and a publish that would land on an existing directory), `json-schema.js`
(`exclusiveMinimum`/`exclusiveMaximum` — the two keywords no spec here happened to use — and the
recursion into `additionalProperties` subschemas), and `scene-patch.js`'s rules that need the SCENE (an
id that cannot be an id, an entity declaring an asset nobody ingested, a camera that does not exist).
`paths.js` 18 → 10, `json-schema.js` 15 → 9, `scene-patch.js` 13 → 12.

── ROUND 55: THE RENDER LOOP (§67) ──
`_launchRenderer` starts a renderer and hands the rest to `_driveRender`; the M3 suites drive that end to
end with a REAL Blender, so they only ever see what a healthy machine produces. With the runtime stubbed
and a real store underneath, the branches around it are all reachable: a composition with **no `jobs`
service**, a `jobs.start` that **throws** (the render is unaffected and the record must say so), a render
cancelled **between the record write and the spawn** (the child exists and must not be left behind), the
child's journal (frames, a FAILED frame, a line that is not JSON), a render killed with frames still owed,
a delivery whose video disagrees with the job, and a full disk. `host/lib/index.js` 167 → 132.

Two notes about what this round did NOT assert, both deliberate: the operator-facing `ctx.logger.warn` on
a projection failure (the logger is the harness's own property, not a service a test can provide — the
DURABLE warning is asserted instead, and it is the one a reader depends on), and the four torn-journal
conditions, which belong to `invalidate…`/`incompleteJournalWarning`'s own suite (`render-journal.test.mjs`)
because the function takes all four as parameters for exactly that reason.

── ROUND 54: WHAT THE THREE PLANES SAY WITH A DEPENDENCY MISSING (§66) ──
Every branch here is a sentence a model or an operator reads on a machine that is not the developer's:
no attachment store (so the image cannot be shown and its path is named instead), no approval service
(so nobody can be asked), a probe that failed (returned as a STRUCTURED error so the settings card
renders the reason), and no host bundle at all (so the four M3 tools describe the DEPLOYMENT rather
than the request). `tool/lib/shared.js` 22 → **0**, `tool/lib/render-tools.js` 19 → 11,
`ui/lib/index.js` 25 → 13.

Three refusals stay dark ON PURPOSE and are named in the test header: an oversized body, a body that is
not a JSON object, and the HTTP-level `UI_REQUEST_FAILED` wrap. All three sit behind the HTTP dispatch,
which only the browser suite reaches; reproducing them would mean standing up a server to test a
three-line wrapper.

── ROUND 53: THE STORE'S ERROR PATHS (§65) ──
Three files, 125 dark lines, and they are all the state a HEALTHY store never produces: a project
directory with no record ("it was never completed"), a record written by a build from the future, a
revision directory with no spec, a job whose status transition is illegal, a patch that would produce
an invalid scene, a preview requested on a revision whose compile produced no checkpoint. Each case
writes the document that provokes the branch — including documents a correct writer would never
produce, because "what does this build do with a store another build wrote" is a question only an
artificially broken store can answer. `project-store.js` 47 → 12, `revision-transaction.js` 71 → 39,
`render-job-store.js` 22 → **0**; the product total went 543 → 454.

Two behaviours are worth more than the rest. `unfinished()` distinguishes ABSENT from CORRUPT: a job
directory with no record is surfaced as `{jobId, record: null}`, while a record that exists but is not
JSON makes the whole scan throw ("Refusing to treat corruption as absence") — neither may read as
"nothing unfinished", which is the one answer restart recovery must never give. And the render job store
REFUSES an illegal status transition: round 47 learned the table by being refused, and a record in a
state no reader can act on is worse than an error.

── ROUND 52: CANCELLING A RENDER, AND REFUSING TO PUBLISH A VIDEO THAT LIES (§64) ──
The two ends of a delivery that went wrong. `cancelJob` answers three kinds of job — an M1 attempt log
(nothing to cancel), a render whose handle is in THIS process (the provider's terminate ladder), and a
render whose process belongs to a previous Host (signal the group directly) — and it distinguishes "the
cancel was requested" from "the process was signalled" from "the process is GONE", measuring the last
one. `_deliverJob` refuses to encode an incomplete frame set (encoding "whatever is there" is how a
delivery ships 447 of 450 frames) and refuses to publish a video whose PROBED properties disagree with
the job's claims, leaving `failed` and a code on the record in both cases. `_deliverJob` is at ZERO dark
lines and `host/lib/index.js` went 220 → 167.

── ROUND 51: THE PROVIDER'S ACTION SURFACE (§63) ──
What was left dark in `provider-local` after the bootstrap round were the ten refusals at the TOP of the
actions (a missing checkpoint path, an empty frame list, a view with no id, a SceneSpec path that is not
a path) plus `resolveEngineKey` — the decision about which engine a render will REALLY use — and the
warnings a capability probe derives from a probe result. None of it needs Blender: the refusals happen
before the spawn, and the engine decision reads a capabilities document the stub writes.
`provider-local/lib/index.js` 124 → 38.

Three of those refusals are worth more than the rest: an empty frame list is refused with the reason
("would report success while writing nothing"), an engine that is not assignable is downgraded WITH a
warning naming both engines, and the Blender 5.2.1 trap (assignable but absent from the static enum) is
warned about separately because availability must be decided behaviourally (D1). Nothing was wrong with
any of them — this round read them, it did not change them.

── ROUND 50: THE PROVIDER'S BOOTSTRAP PATH, AND AN ERROR CODE THE CONTRACT DOES NOT CONTAIN (§62) ──
`runBootstrap` is the single door every Blender invocation goes through, and 46 of its lines were dark
because the suite that exercises it needs a REAL Blender — which produces exactly one of its branches.
The other nine (a missing bootstrap script, an unresolvable executable, a spawn that throws, a DEADLINE,
a caller CANCELLATION, a process that dies without writing a result, a result that is not JSON, a result
in the wrong protocol version, a result that reports an error) each carry their own stable code and are
now driven through a stub `subprocess` over real files on disk. `provider-local` 168 → 124.

The defect this found is a code-space one. `normaliseErrorCode` prefixed EVERY bare code bootstrap.py
reports, which is right for the Blender family (`UNSUPPORTED_ACTION` -> `BLENDER_UNSUPPORTED_ACTION`) and
wrong for the DOMAIN family: the Python side's most common failure, `SCENE_VALIDATION_FAILED` (27 sites),
reached the model as `BLENDER_SCENE_VALIDATION_FAILED` — a code that is not in `BlenderErrorCode` at all,
so nothing could branch on it, `recovery.md`'s index by code could not find it, and the same failure
carried two different codes depending on which layer noticed it. The prefix is now applied only when the
contract exposes that prefixed form; a known bare code passes through; an unknown one becomes
`SCRIPT_ERROR`, which is what "no code to branch on" means.

── ROUND 49: THE DELIVERY ENCODER, DRIVEN BY A STUB SUBPROCESS (§61) ──
`video-encoder.js` was 49 dark lines, and every one of them is a state a WORKING ffmpeg cannot
produce: "exit 0 but no file", "an empty file", "output that is not JSON", "no video stream", "the
process was killed instead of exiting". A real encode suite can only ever see the happy path — which is
how round 30 shipped a delivery that failed to encode while its record still said "encoding". The seam
is `ctx.get('subprocess')`, so it is a stub here: the encoder's own logic is real, the files it stats
are real, and each branch is one object away. The file is now at **zero**.

Two rules got pinned while doing it, both of which the code argues for in its own comments: ffprobe's
`nb_read_frames` (a measurement, from `-count_frames`) must beat `nb_frames` (the container's claim),
and the argv is an ARRAY with the input options before `-i` and `-frames:v` after it — the placement
ffmpeg 8.0.1 refuses to accept any other way.

── ROUND 48: THE REMOTE HALF OF ASSET INGEST (§60) ──
`_fetchAssetToScratch` (13 lines) was dark for a reason worth stating: it uses the global `fetch`, so
its failures look unreachable from a contract test — and a test that needs the network is a test nobody
runs. It is not unreachable. A `node:http` server on a random loopback port is the real thing: the host
fetches over a real socket, streams a real body, and hits its real HTTP and size branches, with nothing
mocked and nothing leaving the machine. `host/lib/index.js` 244 → 220, and the fetch half is at zero.

What remains in `ingestAsset` is its POST-COPY size check, which can only fire if the source file grows
between the two `stat` calls — a race guard, named in the test header as deliberately uncovered rather
than left to look like a gap. The rest of the refusals (nothing to import, a path that is a DIRECTORY,
a local file above the cap) are now driven too, and a mutation proved the checks tell the two cap sites
apart: removing the pre-copy one makes the POST-copy message appear, which the assertion rejects.

── ROUND 47: THE DELIVERY RANGE AND THE RECOVERY PASS (§59) ──
Both are host-level wrappers around logic that is already tested one layer down — `frameNumbers` in the
contracts, `reconcileRenderJob` in `contract/render-reconciler.test.mjs` — and a wrapper is where a
refusal becomes a CODE, where a request is narrowed and the narrowing is REPORTED, and where one broken
job must not stop the pass over the others. `host/lib/index.js` 291 → 244.

Writing them found a defect of the same family as round 45's `r0000`: a frame list containing `null`
acquired a frame the caller never wrote, because `Number(null)` is `0` — and the refusal that followed
said frame 0 was "outside the project's range" instead of "there were no usable frame numbers".
`Number('')` and `Number([])` are 0 as well, so the coercion is now explicit about what counts.

Two lines are left in these two clusters and both are DEAD rather than dark, named rather than implied:
`renderPreview`'s "rendered from an inherited checkpoint" sentence (the resolved checkpoint is always
replaced by the one compiled for the requested revision) and the recovery pass's
`previous === null` writer arm (`unfinishedAcross` only yields entries that HAVE a record).

── ROUND 46: WHAT HAPPENS AROUND A RENDER (§58) ──
`renderPreview` and `renderViews` HAVE real suites — which is exactly why 84 of their lines had never
run: a suite with a working Blender only ever sees the happy path. What was dark is every decision
around the render (a missing preview profile, a sample budget that reduces a request and says so, a
subject guessed from several candidates, a renderer that reports success without bytes, and the failed
job record a broken render must leave behind). The runtime is the seam, so it is a stub here over a
REAL store, and the PNGs it hands back are real PNGs because the host composes a contact sheet out of
them. `host/lib/index.js` 374 → 291; `renderViews` and its plan are at zero.

One line is left in `renderPreview`, and it is DEAD rather than dark: the provenance sentence has a
branch for "this revision was rendered from the r0001 checkpoint because it has none of its own", but
the resolved checkpoint is always replaced by the one compiled for the requested revision, so the
condition cannot hold. The check pins the fact that makes it dead rather than pretending to cover it.

── ROUND 45: THE HOST'S READS THAT NEED NO BLENDER (§57) ──
`getRevisionDetail`, `readRevisionPair` and the patch dry run inside `validateScene` were 55 dark
lines between them — the reads the workbench is built on. The fixture that reaches them is a revision
committed with `saveCheckpoint: false`: a documented, legitimate state (the SceneSpec is the source of
truth; the .blend is derived), committed through the host's own transaction with a runtime that throws
if anything touches it. `host/lib/index.js` 429 → 374, and all three are at zero.

Two things came out of it. The smaller one is a real defect: `readRevisionPair({ to: 'r0001' })`
passed the internal `GENESIS_REVISION` sentinel (`r0000`) to the store, so "the first revision has
nothing before it" was answered with `REVISION_ID_INVALID: "r0000" is not a revision id` — an error
about an id the caller never wrote.

The larger one is a measurement about the reading itself: the mutation that removes
`manifest.previews ?? []` SURVIVED, because the manifest writer always writes that array. That is the
D125 shape (a rule shadowed by another), but the opposite conclusion applies — that fallback guards a
store written by an OLDER build, which is a state that still exists, so the fix was to produce it
(delete the artifact lists from the manifest and read again) rather than to delete the guard.

── ROUND 44: THE PRODUCT'S OWN MODEL-FACING COMPONENT (§56) ──
The single largest cluster left in `host/lib/index.js` was `createVisualReviewer()` — 55 lines, and not
one of them had run: reaching it needed a real model call on a real render. It resolves `llm` and
`attachments` per call, so stubbing those two makes every guard deterministic, and the guards are the
reason it mattered: a missing service, a failed model call, a persisted sheet, one message carrying the
prompt AND the image, and above all **an empty answer, which is a FAILURE and not "a scene with nothing
wrong"** — the two look identical downstream, and reporting the second as the first would let a broken
reviewer silently approve every scene it was shown.

`host/lib/index.js` went 493 → 429; the whole reviewer, including the prompt builder and the answer
parser (both of which had dark branches of their own), is now at zero. What is left in the host is
thirteen methods, and they are the M1–M3 pipeline itself: `renderPreview` (43), `_deliverJob` (36),
`_resolveDeliveryRange` (29), `_launchRenderer` (26), `validateScene` (22), `cancelJob` (21),
`_driveRender` (21), `readRevisionPair` (20), `reconcileRenderJobs` (19), `_renderViewPlan` (18),
`ingestAsset` (18), `renderViews` (13). Each needs a Blender or a job store rather than a service seam,
which is why they are still here.

── ROUNDS 41–43: THE TOOL PLANE WENT TO ZERO (§54, §55) ──
`tool/tools.js` 51 → **0**, `tool/visual-tools.js` 11 → **0**, `tool/index.js` 17 → **0**,
`tool/render-tools.js` 33 → 19. The tool plane is the surface a model actually touches, and three of
its four files now have no unexecuted line at all: the failure paths that a working host can never
produce are driven by a stub host instead (`contract/tool-plane-output.test.mjs`).

What is left in the plane is two files and both are named: `render-tools.js`'s remaining 19 lines are
the M3 JOB guards (a tool called with no host service, an encode that disagrees with the job's own
claims, a cancel that throws) and `shared.js`'s 22 are the approval-and-attachment helpers reached only
by tools that need a real approval plane. Neither is dark because nobody looked; both need a
composition this layer does not build.

The other finding of the pair is recorded in §55/D128: one of the new assertions compared a result's
error code against a constant that does not exist, so it compared `undefined` to `undefined` and
passed. The check that caught it was the broadest one in the file — "no prose leaks a JavaScript
value" — which is worth remembering the next time a specific assertion looks convincing.

── ROUND 41: THE TOOL PLANE'S FAILURE PATHS, AND A WORD THE CONTRACT DOES NOT CONTAIN (§54) ──
`tool/lib/tools.js` was 51 dark lines with a very regular shape: six `catch` blocks that turn a host
failure into a stable code, eight `presentCall` card titles, and three notes only one state produces.
The reason was structural — the only suite that drives these tools needs a WORKING host, so it can
only ever produce successful calls. `contract/tool-plane-output.test.mjs` puts a stub host under them
instead (103 coverage files now, one more suite), and the file went 51 → 0.

The check on those card titles found a real defect the moment it ran: `presentCall()`'s `kind` is a
CLOSED vocabulary owned by `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tools`, and six call sites in this package said
`kind: 'write'` — a word that vocabulary does not contain. The check now PARSES the vocabulary out of
the installed `presentation.d.ts`, so the list cannot rot into our own copy of someone else's contract.

What remains in the tool plane is two shapes, named rather than implied: the review tool's own failure
catch (`VISUAL_REVIEW_FAILED`, visual-tools.js 3 lines) and the capability probe's failure catch
(index.js 17 lines). Both need a host that throws in a place no current case makes it throw.

── ROUND 40: THREE PROSE BLOCKS A MODEL READS, AND THE BUG THE MUTATION ANCHOR FOUND (§53) ──
`tool/lib/visual-tools.js` was third on that list at 69 dark lines, and the reason was structural: the
three blocks the tool HANDS A MODEL (a review's notes, a repair loop's round log, the two lines that
describe one measured issue) were written inline in `execute` bodies, where the only way to reach a
branch that has content is a real render PLUS a real model call. Extracting them into pure functions
and driving them from the REAL `scoreReview` / `validateFindings` / `runVisualLoop` results took the
file from 69 dark lines to 11 (and to 3 this round), and the contract layer from 61 to 101 checks.

The 11 that remain are two shapes, both named rather than implied:

  - three `presentCall` bodies (lines 164-167, 439-442 and the review's own) — the card title a tool
    hands the UI plane. Reachable when a session renders the card, which no suite does;
  - the review tool's own failure branch (239-241) — `VISUAL_REVIEW_FAILED`, which needs a real render
    that fails.

And the extraction itself is where the round's most useful finding came from: the mutation driver
refuses an anchor that appears twice, and three of them did. The reason was a REAL duplication —
`blender_visual_autofix` kept appending the handover block after it had been extracted, so a run would
have shown the same handover twice. No suite could see it, because nothing executed the tool. One does
now, and it asserts the handover reaches the model exactly once.

── WHAT THE RULE DOES AND DOES NOT SAY ──
A line is COVERED when the innermost range covering its FIRST CODE CHARACTER has a positive count.
That keeps everything the block tree was for, and drops the false dark lines:

  - a never-called function's body: its own range (count 0) is innermost at every line start inside it,
    so those lines stay dark — this is the case the probe exists for;
  - a branch that never ran and has a line of its own: the branch's zero range covers that line's
    first code character, so it stays dark;
  - a line whose ternary arm was never taken: the zero range starts after the statement start, so the
    line reads as executed — which it was;
  - the module wrapper (count 1, the whole file) is the OUTERMOST range and never wins inside a
    function, or every loaded file would read as fully covered.

IT IS NOT BRANCH COVERAGE, and that price is stated rather than hidden: an untaken arm on a line that
ran is invisible here. What stays visible is any body or branch with a line of its own.

── THE BLIND SPOT THAT WAS NOT ONE, AND HOW IT WAS FOUND (§42) ──
From round 23 this file said: "`dsh web` writes ZERO coverage reports, so every path reached only
through the UI reads as dark — that is why listProjects, getRevisionDetail, getQaRecord and
readArtifact report count 0 although the browser suite exercises all four." Every clause of that was
measured and true. The CAUSE was never measured, and it was the test:

    $ NODE_V8_COVERAGE=… node deepblend/tests/e2e/ui.e2e.mjs     # the harness's 300 ms grace
    coverage reports: 2, mentioning a product package: 0

    $ … send SIGTERM yourself and WAIT
    SIGTERM -> exited after 717 ms with code=0 signal=null        # it stops willingly
    coverage reports: 1 -> 2                                     # 28 product modules in that one

`dsh` installs a SIGTERM handler that disposes the app fiber and exits cleanly; the harness waited
300 ms and then SIGKILLed, killing the process in the middle of its own shutdown. Killing a server
that is willing to stop measures the kill path, not the shutdown path — and what it silently threw
away was the testability of a whole plane.

What the four methods look like now (this reading): their signature lines are covered, and 1-3 lines
each remain dark inside their sparser error branches. `ui-api.js` went from 61 dark lines (round 24)
to 13. The UI suite now asserts the server stopped on its own (`via: 'sigterm'`), so a shutdown that
gets slower goes red instead of quietly costing coverage again.

Read a dark line as "no test I can see executes this", not as "nothing executes this" — and when a
reading says "I cannot see this", ask what ended the process before filing it as permanent.

── WHAT THE TOOL NOW REFUSES TO DO (each one learned by getting it wrong, §37, §40 and §42) ──
It exits non-zero and keeps its evidence when the SUITE exited non-zero, echoing the failing lines: a
reading of a red tree is not a reading, and the version that threw the suite's output away left
`suite exit code: 1` as the only trace of a 15-minute failure with no way to see which suite it was.

It exits non-zero and names the files when a PRODUCT SOURCE FILE CHANGED while the suite ran. Coverage
is line numbers from the processes read against source text from disk, and those are two different
moments: a run that overlapped an edit reported a function called seven times by a test as "never
executed", and listed comment lines as the dark ones.

And it no longer reports a line as dark because one arm of it was not taken — the four wrong merge
rules are listed in `tools/coverage-merge.mjs`, and a fifth is now a test failure rather than a
reading nobody audits. The fifth was not in the merge at all: it was the harness killing the server
it had started (§42).
